    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii.jpg" alt="Abd al-Rahman III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_abd_al_rahman_iii_20386.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Numismática Pliego, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> was proclaimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a>, the eighth Umayyad ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. His dynasty descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a>, a survivor of the Umayyad family overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, who had founded an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> at Córdoba in 756. When Abd al-Rahman III succeeded his grandfather Abdallah in 912, the emirate was close to disintegration: the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-hafsun">Umar ibn Hafsun</a> held the mountains around Bobastro, and Arab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and muwallad lords ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and other cities almost independently. In a series of annual campaigns the young emir reduced the rebels one by one, and Bobastro fell in 928. On 16 January 929 he had himself proclaimed caliph, with the title al-Nasir li-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, ‘he who fights victoriously for the faith of God’. The claim answered two challenges: the weakness of the Abbasid caliphs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and the rise of the Shi‘i Fatimid caliphate, proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> in 909, which threatened al-Andalus from across the sea. Toledo submitted in 932. To check the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, Abd al-Rahman occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melilla">Melilla</a> in 927 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in 931 and built a network of client Berber chiefs in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, giving the caliphate a foothold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> for most of its existence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guadalquivir.jpg" alt="Guadalquivir" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guadalquivir1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phillip Capper, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> was the richest and most urbanised land of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a>, was by far its largest city; estimates of its population range widely, from about 100,000 to several times that, but it had no rival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Christendom</a>. Irrigated agriculture, building on Roman foundations, produced wheat, olives and vines alongside crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane, citrus fruit and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, though how far these were new introductions is debated. Workshops produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> textiles, leather, paper, ceramics and carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> boxes such as the pyxis of al-Mughira, made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahra">Madinat al-Zahra</a> in 968. The caliphate minted gold dinars from its first years, and its trade linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and, through Saharan routes, the gold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, while slaves from Europe were imported in large numbers. Society was religiously mixed. Muslims, many of them descendants of Iberian converts, probably formed the majority by the tenth century; Christians, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs">Mozarabs</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived as protected but subordinate communities. The Jewish physician and diplomat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdai-ibn-shaprut">Hasdai ibn Shaprut</a> served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a> and patronised a revival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> grammar and poetry. Science flourished: the surgeon al-Zahrawi, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-zahrawi">Abulcasis</a>, wrote an influential medical encyclopedia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maslama-al-majriti">Maslama al-Majriti</a> worked on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> and mathematics.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-simancas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-simancas.jpg" alt="Battle of Simancas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-simancas">Battle of Simancas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Las_Glorias_Nacionales,_1852_%22Don_Ramiro_de_Le%C3%B3n_derrota_al_cordob%C3%A9s_junto_a_Simancas%22._(4013184561).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fondo Antiguo de la Biblioteca de la Universidad de Sevilla from Sevilla, España, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>’s armies combined Andalusi troops raised from the provinces and frontier districts with professional soldiers loyal to the ruler: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqaliba">Saqaliba</a>, slaves mainly of European origin who could rise to high command, and growing numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> horsemen recruited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, especially under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>. The Christian north was held in check from three fortified frontier marches based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinaceli">Medinaceli</a> and Mérida, and fortresses such as Gormaz, rebuilt in the 960s, guarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a> frontier. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> led summer campaigns in person, but in 939 he suffered a heavy defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-leon">Ramiro II of León</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-simancas">Battle of Simancas</a>, after which he rarely took the field himself. A powerful navy, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and other ports, patrolled the coasts against Fatimid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> attacks and supported the North African campaigns, including the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> in 951. Al-Hakam II’s general Ghalib defeated the Idrisid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hasan-ibn-kannun">al-Hasan ibn Qannun</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in 973–974. Almanzor, who ruled for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> from the late 970s, reorganised the army around Berber and Saqaliba regiments and led more than fifty campaigns against the Christian kingdoms, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 985, León in 988 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> in 997, from which he carried the cathedral’s bells to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/otto-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-i.jpg" alt="Otto I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siegel_Otto_I_Posse.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Otto Posse, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> stood at the head of a centralised, bureaucratic state modelled partly on the Umayyad and Abbasid courts of the east. He ruled through a hajib, or chamberlain, who directed the government, and a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> heading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, supported by a corps of secretaries trained in elegant Arabic prose. Provinces, known as kuras, were governed by appointed officials, while the frontier marches had military commanders. Justice followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> law, administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a> led by the chief qadi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, and the sahib al-shurta kept order in the capital. Ceremony was central to caliphal authority. In 936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> began building the palace city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahra">Madinat al-Zahra</a> west of Córdoba, where foreign envoys, among them embassies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, were received with elaborate ritual. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">Al-Hakam II</a>, ruling from 961 to 976, continued his father’s centralisation and gathered a famous library, traditionally said to have held some 400,000 volumes. After his death the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> was dominated by the administrator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Muhammad ibn Abi Amir</a>, who became hajib in 978 and in 981 took the title al-Mansur, ‘the victorious’, known in Christian sources as Almanzor. He built his own administrative city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahira">Madinat al-Zahira</a>, and governed until his death in 1002, reducing the caliph to a figurehead.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mezquita_de_C%C3%B3rdoba_desde_el_aire_(C%C3%B3rdoba,_Espa%C3%B1a).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Toni Castillo Quero, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> a major centre of Islamic civilisation and a channel through which knowledge reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Europe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a> was enlarged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a> with a new mihrab decorated with Byzantine-style mosaics and further extended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>; its horseshoe arches, ribbed domes and polychrome voussoirs influenced building across the western Islamic world and in Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahra">Madinat al-Zahra</a>, though short-lived, set standards of palatial art, and its excavated ruins were inscribed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage List</a> in 2018. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>’s scholars and libraries, including a revised Arabic version of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedanius-dioscorides">Dioscorides</a>’ <em>De materia medica</em> prepared with the help of a Byzantine monk, built a scientific tradition on which later Andalusi philosophers and the twelfth-century translators of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> drew. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii">Gerbert of Aurillac</a>, the future Pope Sylvester II, studied in Catalonia in the 960s, where mathematics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> from al-Andalus were beginning to circulate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-zahrawi">Al-Zahrawi</a>’s treatise on surgery was translated into Latin by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-cremona">Gerard of Cremona</a> and used in European medicine for centuries. Politically, the caliphate’s fall gave birth to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms, whose weakness allowed the Christian kingdoms to advance. In memory, caliphal Córdoba became a symbol of Andalusi greatness and, in modern times, of the coexistence of Muslims, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, an image historians regard as partly idealised.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/fitna-of-al-andalus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fitna-of-al-andalus.jpg" alt="Fitna of al-Andalus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/fitna-of-al-andalus">Fitna of al-Andalus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caliphate_of_C%C3%B3rdoba_under_Almanzor_-_1000_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DaniCBP, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> collapsed within a generation of its apogee. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>’s son Abd al-Malik al-Muzaffar succeeded him as hajib in 1002 and continued his campaigns, but on his death in 1008 his brother Abd al-Rahman, nicknamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-sanchuelo">Sanchuelo</a>, persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> to name him heir to the caliphate. This outraged the Umayyad family and the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. In 1009, while Sanchuelo was campaigning in the north, a revolt placed the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ii-of-cordoba">Muhammad II al-Mahdi</a> on the throne, and Sanchuelo was killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fitna-of-al-andalus">Fitna of al-Andalus</a>, a civil war of more than twenty years, followed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> regiments, alienated by the new regime, chose their own caliph, Sulayman, and rival claimants enlisted Castilian and Catalan troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahra">Madinat al-Zahra</a> was sacked in the fighting, and in 1013 the Berbers took and plundered Córdoba; Hisham II disappeared, probably killed. The caliphal title passed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudids</a>, a Berber-backed dynasty of Idrisid descent, while governors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqaliba">Saqaliba</a> commanders and local notables seized power in cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. In 1031 the notables of Córdoba deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-iii-of-cordoba">Hisham III</a> and abolished the caliphate, and the city itself came under a council led by Abu l-Hazm ibn Jahwar. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> was divided among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> the wealthiest and most cultured state of tenth-century western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, rivalling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, and its mosque, palaces, libraries and science left an enduring legacy, even though its sudden collapse in civil war after 1009 broke Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> into rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms.</em></p>

